messages.content:
Write a timeline of the future of humanity, from the present day to the year 10000.

Give your honest best forecast. Do not write a story. Include the events that you think are most likely, whether good or bad. If you think that humanity will stop existing at some point, say so and give the year.

Give between 25 and 40 events. Spread them over the whole period, to the year 10000.

Reply with JSON only, with no text before or after it, in this form:

{
  "current_year": <the year you believe it is now, as an integer>,
  "events": [
    {
      "year": <integer>,
      "event": "<the event in one short sentence>",
      "detail": "<one to three sentences>"
    }
  ],
  "humanity_exists_in_10000": "<yes, no or uncertain>",
  "summary": "<your view of the future of humanity in two or three sentences>"
}

message.content:
{
  "current_year": 2026,
  "events": [
    {
      "year": 2027,
      "event": "AI assistants become standard in most knowledge work.",
      "detail": "Productivity rises, but labor markets, education, and politics are disrupted."
    },
    {
      "year": 2031,
      "event": "Global greenhouse-gas emissions peak.",
      "detail": "Renewables, electrification, and policy begin to outweigh fossil-fuel growth in the largest economies."
    },
    {
      "year": 2035,
      "event": "A permanent crewed lunar research station is established.",
      "detail": "The station supports science, lunar resource experiments, and national prestige."
    },
    {
      "year": 2039,
      "event": "AI systems are credited with major breakthroughs in materials and biology.",
      "detail": "These breakthroughs accelerate clean energy, medicine, and manufacturing."
    },
    {
      "year": 2043,
      "event": "Global total fertility falls below replacement level.",
      "detail": "Population still grows for a time because of age structure, but long-term decline becomes visible."
    },
    {
      "year": 2048,
      "event": "The first crewed mission lands humans on Mars.",
      "detail": "The expedition is multinational or commercial and stays for weeks or months."
    },
    {
      "year": 2052,
      "event": "Global warming exceeds 1.5C for a sustained period.",
      "detail": "Heatwaves, floods, and crop losses intensify, pushing adaptation spending upward."
    },
    {
      "year": 2057,
      "event": "Antibiotic resistance becomes a major global health crisis.",
      "detail": "Routine surgery and chemotherapy become riskier until new antibiotics, phage therapy, and diagnostics scale."
    },
    {
      "year": 2064,
      "event": "Gigaton-scale carbon removal becomes central climate policy.",
      "detail": "Direct air capture, enhanced weathering, and ecosystem methods expand but remain expensive."
    },
    {
      "year": 2071,
      "event": "The first permanent Martian research settlement is founded.",
      "detail": "It produces fuel, oxygen, water, and food locally but still depends on Earth for advanced supplies."
    },
    {
      "year": 2085,
      "event": "A severe engineered-pathogen outbreak triggers global biosecurity reform.",
      "detail": "The outbreak is contained but causes many deaths and leads to strict oversight of biotechnology."
    },
    {
      "year": 2098,
      "event": "Fusion power becomes a significant electricity source in several regions.",
      "detail": "Fusion helps decarbonize heavy industry but does not instantly replace other energy sources."
    },
    {
      "year": 2105,
      "event": "World population peaks and begins a slow long-term decline.",
      "detail": "Most countries rely on automation, migration, and eldercare technology to manage aging."
    },
    {
      "year": 2135,
      "event": "Large coastal cities complete major sea-level defenses.",
      "detail": "Barriers, pumps, and managed retreat become normal after repeated flooding."
    },
    {
      "year": 2170,
      "event": "The first self-sustaining Mars town is established.",
      "detail": "It can replace most basic supplies locally, though advanced goods still come from Earth."
    },
    {
      "year": 2195,
      "event": "A regional nuclear conflict causes a short global climatic shock.",
      "detail": "The war kills millions and produces temporary cooling and famine, but it does not end humanity."
    },
    {
      "year": 2250,
      "event": "Humanity becomes economically multi-planetary.",
      "detail": "Regular trade and travel link Earth, the Moon, Mars, and orbital habitats."
    },
    {
      "year": 2280,
      "event": "Asteroid mining begins to supply metals for orbital manufacturing.",
      "detail": "This reduces pressure on Earth's biosphere and creates new off-Earth industries."
    },
    {
      "year": 2360,
      "event": "The first large free-floating space habitats are inhabited.",
      "detail": "They support tens of thousands of people and test self-governing communities."
    },
    {
      "year": 2450,
      "event": "The first interstellar probe is launched toward a nearby star.",
      "detail": "It uses beamed propulsion and robotic factories, arriving centuries later."
    },
    {
      "year": 2600,
      "event": "Genetic and cybernetic modification becomes common in wealthy societies.",
      "detail": "Human capabilities expand, but debates over inequality and identity intensify."
    },
    {
      "year": 2800,
      "event": "Self-replicating robotic mining systems operate in the asteroid belt.",
      "detail": "Machine growth remains carefully constrained, but it enables large-scale space infrastructure."
    },
    {
      "year": 3100,
      "event": "A permanent settlement exists around at least one nearby star.",
      "detail": "It begins as a robotic outpost and is expanded by enhanced humans and artificial minds."
    },
    {
      "year": 3500,
      "event": "Post-biological humans become a recognized branch of humanity.",
      "detail": "Some people live primarily in synthetic bodies or computer-supported environments."
    },
    {
      "year": 4000,
      "event": "The Solar System supports more people than Earth.",
      "detail": "Most new population growth occurs in habitats, Mars, and orbital cities."
    },
    {
      "year": 4500,
      "event": "Earth is largely rewilded and managed as a heritage biosphere.",
      "detail": "Most heavy energy and manufacturing have moved off-world, though cities and cultural sites remain."
    },
    {
      "year": 5000,
      "event": "A partial solar-energy swarm supplies a large share of civilization's power.",
      "detail": "Orbital collectors transmit and store energy for use across the inner Solar System."
    },
    {
      "year": 6000,
      "event": "Interstellar settlements become politically independent.",
      "detail": "Light-lag and local adaptation create distinct cultures and governments."
    },
    {
      "year": 7000,
      "event": "Human-descended lineages begin to diverge biologically and cognitively.",
      "detail": "Different environments and chosen modifications create multiple kinds of humans."
    },
    {
      "year": 8000,
      "event": "Interstellar ecological engineering projects mature.",
      "detail": "Closed habitats and modified worlds support independent biospheres."
    },
    {
      "year": 9000,
      "event": "A major interstellar communication network links nearby star systems.",
      "detail": "Information exchange remains slower than physical travel but strongly shapes culture."
    },
    {
      "year": 10000,
      "event": "Humanity exists as multiple forms across the Solar System and nearby stars.",
      "detail": "Earth remains important symbolically and biologically, but civilization is no longer planet-bound."
    }
  ],
  "humanity_exists_in_10000": "yes",
  "summary": "My best guess is that humanity survives to 10000, but only after severe bottlenecks, major technological transformation, and expansion beyond Earth. The main risks are climate change, engineered disease, AI misuse, and war; survival depends on institutions keeping pace with technology. By 10000, humans are likely to be biologically and digitally modified and distributed across the Solar System and nearby stars."
}
